Z||000007||p4n1c||08-01-2001||01:29 PM||||Actually, in my own opinion, Chilton manuals blow.  I have one for my Cutlass and there is a lot of missed information and a lot of wrong information, the Haynes-your-way manuals are the same thing, a friend has one for his Corsica, it's missing a lot of detail. <P>Best bet is to get a factory service manual, study it and you'll be able to take the car apart and put it back together from scratch.<P>------------------<BR>-=-=-=[ p4n1c ]=-=-=-<BR>1988 Olds Cutlass Supreme International<BR>2.8L MPFI V6/TH440-T4<BR>*Under Restoration*||||reg||1
Z||000008||Songman||08-01-2001||01:46 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by fierobaby:<BR><B>but the lines were $170 plus pads and rotters so its over $200 that without labor...</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Wow! That is a lot. Guess you can't use the cheaper Cavalier stuff on your year model. Did you shop around? The lines and everything for my 88 were not that expensive. I just want you to be able to keep your car.<P><BR> <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by fierobaby:<BR><B>and songman.... which kind of cone? we have Sugar, Regular, or Waffle</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><BR>Regular... I'm easy to please! hehe<BR><P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/songman_1.gif"> <BR><A HREF="http://members.home.com/tnfieros" TARGET=_blank>Tennessee Fiero Owners</A>||||reg||1
Z||000009||kslish||08-01-2001||02:18 PM||||  <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Songman:<BR><B> Wow! That is a lot. Guess you can't use the cheaper Cavalier stuff on your year model. Did you shop around? The lines and everything for my 88 were not that expensive. I just want you to be able to keep your car.<BR></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>She's talking about the steel brake lines.  Her's failed due to rust or something.  Those aren't cheap when the time comes to replace them, unless you know how to bend and flare brake lines properly so you can use cheap bulk straight line.  Cost me $140 to do because I bought straight tubing and had them professionally bent and flaired so I could install them.  Basically took the old line to the shop and told them, I want this (the new tubing) to look like this (the old tubing).<P>I believe fierobaby bought the pre-formed lines which are a little more.  I probably would have went that direction, but then I would have missed the Carlisle show due to waiting for them to be delivered (lines went 4 days before the show).<P>------------------<BR>Ken S.<BR>'87GT and '86SC<BR>See them at <A HREF="http://homepage.mac.com/kjslish/Fiero.html" TARGET=_blank>http://homepage.mac.com/kjslish/Fiero.html</A><BR>Western PA Fiero Club - <A HREF="http://www.westpennfiero.org" TARGET=_blank>www.westpennfiero.org</A><p>[This message has been edited by kslish (edited 08-01-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000010||JohnnyK||08-01-2001||02:31 PM||||$170? Jesus.... It cost me about $20.. (However, I had to endure the pain of going under the car and routing them..) Doesn't look so pretty, but I stop.||||reg||1
Z||000011||Fformula88||08-01-2001||05:46 PM||||Even if money is tight, your not going to find a car that is going to have parts that are much cheaper.  Its a lot for brakes, but any car will eventually need brakes.<P>Its been my experience than Fiero parts are almost always a lot cheaper than the same part on a newer (say 90's) car.  Even cheapo Cavaliers!  Your going to have a lot of trouble finding a cheaper car to operate.||||reg||1
